              {
                "text": "**Analyzing Region Enclosure**\n\nI'm focusing on identifying enclosed regions of green (3) to understand which black (0) pixels should become yellow (4). Example 1 shows this. The closed regions of green appear to define the conversion. I'm exploring how these closed regions dictate which 0s become 4s. I am working on how to generalize that.\n\n\n**Defining Enclosure Conditions**\n\nI'm now investigating specific cases to define enclosure. The key is to determine if a zero can reach the boundary, given that threes are obstacles. Is it 4-connected, or is it 8-connected? The example reveals an 8-connected boundary. I am considering if this approach can be generalised.\n\n\n**Investigating Connectivity Obstacles**\n\nI'm now diving into the implications of 4- vs. 8-connectivity. In example 1, the key zero at (11,4) is 4-connected, but cannot connect to the boundary. The large enclosed zero region is also 4-connected. However, I am now thinking about how the boundary of this loop is connected, looking at diagonal obstructions in Example 5.\n\n\n**Refining Enclosure Criteria**\n\nI'm now focusing on specific coordinate examples to refine enclosure criteria. The recent exploration of Example 2's (14,18) and (15,18) revealed that 4-connectivity, not 8, matters, because their neighbors are all 3s. This is key, as these black pixels cannot escape. I am now trying to generalize this new rule.\n\n\n**Validating Region Fill**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on a critical rule: any 4-connected component of zeros that doesn't touch the boundary becomes fours. However, I am now focused on a key case in Example 3. I am tracing those fours to check if the rule holds! I'm now trying to validate this specific rule through thorough inspection of Example 3.\n\n\n**Confirming Flood Fill Solution**\n\nI've got it! I realized it's all about 4-connectivity flood fill from the grid boundary. Any zero unreachable via this method gets converted to a four. I tested it with specific examples. I am now checking if this is standard flood-fill. It appears so!\n\n\n",
